{
  "configuration_id": "config_013",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_003 (multimodal, scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l, concat) achieved 0.532 acc vs config_011 (RNA-only, scvi_n200) at 0.520 acc — a modest ~1.2% gain. This small difference raises uncertainty about whether the image modality is genuinely contributing complementary information or whether the gain is within noise. Question: config_013 (image_only, singleprofile, imagenet_vit_l) is a clean ablation that isolates the image encoder's standalone contribution using the exact same image encoder and profile setting as config_003, with RNA removed. If image_only performs poorly (<0.45), the multimodal gain is likely a small complementary effect and we should focus budget on RNA-side improvements (e.g., multiprofile, encoder choice). If image_only performs competitively (>0.50), the image is a strong standalone signal and the multimodal combination may be suboptimal, pointing us toward better fusion strategies. Why this is the best next experiment: before spending limited budget on more complex multimodal variants (multiprofile, weighted fusion, transformer aggregation), we must first confirm that the image modality is actually adding meaningful information. This single-variable ablation maximally reduces uncertainty about the most fundamental design decision (whether to include image features at all) and will directly guide whether subsequent experiments should exploit the multimodal direction or pivot to RNA-side optimizations."
}

